    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201702047748_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Part Time Faculty Math/Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2024-04-23\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nAbout Harrisburg University Harrisburg University offers an applied science and technology education for students who want a degree and a dedicated pathway to a career. Its focus is to educate career-minded individuals in the areas of science, technology, engineering and math (STEM careers), giving them the tools to succeed, while helping to shape their communities, locally and beyond. Our goal is to help stimulate economic growth in our region by establishing a STEM educated workforce — a necessary resource in today's economy.  Mission: The Harrisburg University of Science and Technology offers innovative academic and research programs in science and technology that respond to local and global needs. The institution fosters a diverse community of learners, provides access and support to students who want to pursue a career in science and technology, and supports business creation and economic development.  ----------------------------------------------------------  We are seeking dedicated part-time instructors committed to fostering student success and engagement in our Engineering and Math Sciences program across multiple campuses, including Philadelphia and Harrisburg. The ideal candidates will have a passion for teaching first-generation students, a student-centered approach, and expertise in supporting students who may struggle with Math and Physics concepts. Additionally, candidates should be adept at offering skills-based learning opportunities and hands-on experiences in the classroom. This position will support and contribute to multiple Programs at our University.   Key Responsibilities: Create inclusive and supportive learning environments tailored to the needs of first-generation students and learners with varying mathematical and scientific backgrounds. Deliver engaging and interactive instruction in Physics and Applied Mathematics courses (including Statistics, Calculus and Differential Equations) and courses including computational skills including Python, MATLAB, R and Tableau. Implement innovative teaching strategies that emphasize skills-based learning, problem-solving, and real-world applications. Provide individualized support and guidance to students to enhance their understanding and mastery of course content. Design and facilitate hands-on activities, labs, and projects that promote experiential learning and critical thinking skills. Foster a collaborative and student-centered classroom culture that encourages participation, teamwork, and active learning. Assess student progress through a variety of methods and provide constructive feedback to support student growth and development. Specific courses needed are:   College Algebra Calculus I  Calculus II Calculus III  Differential Equations Mathematical Modeling  Introductory Statistics Predictive Modeling Advanced Analytics & reporting (Risk Analysis) University Physics I & II Engineering Mechanics: Statics Engineering Mechanics: Dynamics   Qualifications: Master's degree or higher in Physics, Mathematics, Engineering, or a related field (Ph.D. preferred). Demonstrated teaching experience at the undergraduate level, with a focus on supporting diverse student populations. Strong understanding of pedagogical approaches that promote student engagement, retention, and success. Ability to create inclusive and equitable learning environments that value diverse perspectives and backgrounds. Proficiency in designing and implementing hands-on learning experiences and skills-based assessments. Excellent communication, mentoring, and interpersonal skills to effectively engage with students and colleagues.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9200750178842_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "PT Faculty Information Systems Engineering an",
      "description": "Posted: 2023-02-03\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nAbout ISEM at HU: Harrisburg University is actively seeking a part time, adjunct faculty member with strong teaching skills for the Information Systems Engineering and Management (ISEM) Master’s Program.  The ISEM Program cuts across three active areas of work: Information Systems, Systems Engineering and Management. To accommodate teaching needs in both its Master’s and PhD programs, ISEM is currently seeking one or more faculty members with expertise in research design and methodologies.  Position Summary:  The ideal candidate is exceptionally qualified in the research process and quantitative and qualitative research methods. The candidate will be required to teach research design and methodology courses to Master's and PhD level students. This involves teaching students how to craft a research problem, perform a literature review then plan, schedule and then successfully execute a research project using classic research methods.  Specific areas of interest are as follows: Research Proposal development (GRAD 695). This is the first of the two-course Master’s students’ Capstone experience. The focus in this course is conduct of a literature review, definition of a research problem and development of a plan for execution of a successful research project using classic research methods.  Master’s Thesis development (ISEM 699, GRAD 699). This is the second of the two-course Master’s Capstone experience. In this course, students execute their GRAD 695 Research Proposal and generate their final Master’s Thesis. Research Methods (GRAD 509). This is a required course for incoming ISEM PhD students that prepares the students for PhD level research.  Specific course descriptions are available upon request. See the contact point listed below. Essential Responsibilities:  Commitment to working with diverse student populations at Master’s and PhD academic levels Commitment to equity in education at Master’s and PhD levels Commitment to delivery of quality education in accordance with defined HU, ISEM and specific course requirements Ability to periodically travel to the Harrisburg University Campus at 326 Market Street, Harrisburg, PA 17101 three Saturdays in a 15-week semester to deliver course material face-to-face to students. Qualifications: Interested candidates should have: Earned Ph.D. or equivalent is preferred. Minimum of Master’s degree is required, in an Engineering or Management discipline. Demonstrated teaching experience in Research Methods and Thesis development at the graduate and / or doctoral levels. Preferred experience with research that generates scholarly outputs including conference presentations, refereed journal publications, and other scholarly works. Willingness and aptitude for teaching and utilizing technology in the classroom. Experience working with, mentoring and advising students. Current or prior corporate / industry experience is valued. In addition to the required qualifications above, ideal candidates should have experience with learning management systems (LMS) and online teaching platforms. The required HU LMS technology is Canvas and MS Teams. Interested candidates should apply on the HU website. Specific questions can be forwarded to Dr. Robert Pittman, ISEM Academic Program Lead, at  RPittman@HarrisburgU.edu .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=273936081089_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Part Time/Adjunct Faculty: Chemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2020-04-06\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nAbout Harrisburg University:   \n Harrisburg University offers an applied science and technology education for students who want a degree and a dedicated pathway to a career. Its focus is to educate career-minded individuals in the areas of science, technology, engineering and math (STEM careers), giving them the tools to succeed, while helping to shape their communities, locally and beyond. Our goal is to help stimulate economic growth in our region by establishing a STEM educated workforce — a necessary resource in today's economy.   \n Mission:   \n The Harrisburg University of Science and Technology offers innovative academic and research programs in science and technology that respond to local and global needs. The institution fosters a diverse community of learners, provides access and support to students who want to pursue a career in science and technology, and supports business creation and economic development.   \n ----------------------------------------------------------   \n HU seeks a part-time/adjunct faculty member to teach our undergraduate General Chemistry lectures and labs course for this upcoming Fall 2020 semester, and beyond.   \n    \n CHEM 151 General Chemistry I Lecture   \n Description: This course provides a general introduction to atoms and molecules, stoichiometry, states of matter, solutions, reactions, kinetics and equilibrium which serve as a prerequisite for advanced courses.   \n    \n CHEM 152 General Chemistry I Laboratory   \n Description : Companion laboratory component that illustrates the general introduction to atoms and molecules, stoichiometry, states of matter, solutions, reactions, kinetics and equilibrium which serve as a prerequisite for advanced courses.   \n --------------   \n About the INSC program at HU:   \n The ABET-accredited B.S. in Computer and Information Science program at Harrisburg University of Science and Technology focuses on the science behind the coolest gadgets and gear on the market. It involves the skeletons of today’s most powerful websites, and is the root of tomorrow’s most rewarding careers. Technology at Harrisburg University is more than just computer screens and keyboards. It’s a hands-on study of the complex information systems and peripherals that power our world and shape our future.   \n All CISC undergraduate students will complete the CISC program core courses. The core courses are the program required courses and all students, regardless of their plan of study, should successfully complete all these courses. Then, students work with their academic advisors to craft an appropriate program of study which provides personal training in subject and skill areas that align with their career goals, interests, and personal strengths.   \n Accredited by ABET, our curriculum does not limit the students to a pre-defined set of concentration courses, however, a CISC student must successfully complete 15 credit hours of CISC 300 and 400-level courses with a minimum of 6 credits of 400-level courses to graduate. The offerings of the CISC program provide the students with opportunities to deepen their skills and knowledge of cognate areas in Cyber Security, Artificial Intelligence, and Software Engineering.   \n Qualifications:   \n Interested candidates should provide evidence of experience in teaching kinesthetic learners, instructional development, and assessment. Prior industry experience preferred.    \n Harrisburg University requires dedicated professionals who have the experience and desire to work in an active and collaborative environment that stresses high quality service delivery, entrepreneurial spirit, and close contact with a diverse group of internal and external constituents. HU is committed to the support and encouragement of a multicultural environment and seeks candidates who can make a positive contribution in delivering the HU Mission. _____________________________________   \n   Harrisburg University- At a Glance:   \n · Only science and technology-focused comprehensive university between Philadelphia and Pittsburgh   \n · Private, non-sectarian, nonprofit, urban, teaching university   \n · Located in the city of Harrisburg   \n · Incorporated in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania: December 12, 2001   \n · Chartered by Pennsylvania Department of Education: January 20, 2005   \n · Inaugural class arrived August 29, 2005   \n · Accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education, June 22, 2009   \n · 350 full- and part-time employees   \n · Signature 16-story, $73 million, state-of-the-art Academic Center   \n    \n Student Population:   \n · The University's total student enrollment for undergraduate and graduate degree seeking students: 6,300 as of May 1, 2019   \n · Undergraduate Student Body: 51% female, 55% minority   \n · 98% of our students demonstrate financial need   \n · Ranked in top 20 of US News & World Report’s ethnic diversity category for Best National Liberal Arts Colleges   \n · HU places 92% of its graduates in family-sustaining careers in their fields of study within six months of graduation   \n · HU undergraduate student average debt upon graduating is $7,000 less than the national average student education debt   \n    \n Academics:   \n · Full-time faculty with Ph.D. or Terminal Degree: 100%   \n · Bachelor of Science (B.S.) degrees awarded in Advanced Manufacturing- Analytics- Biotechnology and Biosciences- Computer and Information Sciences- Cybersecurity & Operations Management- Environmental Science- Interactive Media- Management, Entrepreneurship & Business Administration- Geospatial Technologies- Integrative Sciences- IS/IT & Nursing (RN-BSN)   \n · Master of Science Degrees awarded in Analytics- Biotechnology- Human Centered Interaction Design- Information Systems Engineering & Management- Learning Technologies- Computer Information Sciences- Project Management- Pharmaceutical Sciences- Techpreneurship- Disruptive Technologies & Healthcare Informatics   \n · PhD awarded in Data Analytics – first student cohort began in Fall 2017. A PhD in ISEM (Information Systems Engineering Management) started its first student cohort in Summer 2019   \n · Trimester system   \n    \n Institutional Leadership:   \n A 30-member, independent, self-perpetuating Board of Trustees led by an Executive Committee governs Harrisburg University. The Board of Trustees delegates authority for day-to-day operations to senior management.   \n    \n Notes of Distinction:   \n · First private nonprofit science and technology-focused institution chartered in the Commonwealth since Carnegie Mellon University   \n · Educational framework is competency-based, applied and experiential   \n · Offers Central Pennsylvania’s only master’s degrees in IT Project Management or Learning Technologies   \n · The University is a Project Management Institute® registered education provider   \n · Home to the Center for Advanced Entertainment and Learning Technologies, The Government Technology Institute, and The Geospatial Technology Center   \n · Co-PI on NSF-funded ADVANCE grant focused on the recruitment, retention, and development of women academics in science, technology, engineering and mathematics (STEM) disciplines   \n    \n Institutional Affiliations & Partnerships:   \n · American Council on Education (ACE)   \n · Association of Independent Colleges and Universities of Pennsylvania (AICUP)   \n · Coalition of Urban and Metropolitan Universities (CUMU)   \n · National Science Foundation (NSF)   \n · Technology Council of Central Pennsylvania (TCCP)   \n · Consortium agreements with:   \n - Penn State University – USA   \n - St. Francis University – USA   \n - Ramnarian Ruia College - Mumbai, India   \n - Acharya Institutes - Bangalore, India   \n - Universidad Rey Juan Carlos - Madrid, Spain   \n - The University College of the Cayman Islands - Grand Cayman, Cayman Islands   \n - EFREI, Engineering School of Information and Digital Technologies and Esigetel, Digital Systems and Communicating Objects – Paris, France   \n - Universitat Internacional de Catalunya- Barcelona, Spain   \n - Rede das Faculdades de Tecnología e Ciencias - Bahía, Brazil   \n - St. Aloysius College- India   \n - Dayananda Sagar Institutions - Bangalore, India   \n · Articulation agreements with:   \n - Harrisburg Area Community College   \n - Community College of Baltimore County   \n - Lehigh Carbon Community College   \n - Alvernia University   \n - Montgomery College   \n - Community College of Philadelphia",
      "department": ""
    }
